From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Weiyi Feng <fwy1998@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 00:11:23 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] hosted: unblock hw.osenvironment when there is no NVRAM
 controller

On Intel (DEVELOPMENT/DEBUG), sysctl hw.osenvironment waits until
sysctl_unblock_osenvironment() runs, which normally happens in
IOPlatformExpert::registerNVRAMController. BeringiaPlatform publishes
IONVRAM directly (IODTNVRAM is a stub), so it never ran: launchd blocked
forever on its first hw.osenvironment sysctl, right after the ignition
banner (thread dump: _sysctl_osenvironment on osenvironment_initialized).
Call it in BeringiaPlatform::start. With this, launchd completes the
ignition sequence.
---
 iokit/Kernel/BeringiaPlatform.cpp | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/BeringiaPlatform.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/BeringiaPlatform.cpp
index e871467..d560cdf 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/BeringiaPlatform.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/BeringiaPlatform.cpp
@@ -38,6 +38,7 @@
  */
 #include <IOKit/IOPlatformExpert.h>
 #include <machine/machine_routines.h>
+extern "C" void sysctl_unblock_osenvironment(void);
 extern "C" {
 #include <beringia_hosted.h>
 }
@@ -74,6 +75,9 @@ public:
 		 */
 		publishResource("IORTC", this);
 		publishResource("IONVRAM");
+		/* registerNVRAMController also unblocks hw.osenvironment (on Intel, in DEVELOPMENT builds): launchd's
+		 * first sysctl of it waits for that. With no NVRAM controller, the platform must do it itself. */
+		sysctl_unblock_osenvironment();
 		return true;
 	}
 
